window.COURSE_DATA = {"course":{"name":"CS 111 — Computer Science I","term":"Summer 2026","examTitle":"Final Exam — Tues 7/28","examDate":"2026-07-28","window":"8:35 AM–10:15 AM","questions":65,"points":65,"windowMinutes":100,"questionBank":140,"timeLimit":"Canvas displays Time Limit: None, but the exam is available for only 100 minutes.","format":"The syllabus says the real final may mix multiple-choice and short-answer questions, but this requested practice bank uses multiple-choice only.","sourceNote":"Based on the current Canvas course, syllabus, 13 slide decks, the July 23 Arrays and Classes example, and the public college references listed below. All questions are original or substantially adapted.","sourceReferences":[{"title":"Foothill College CS 1A Course Outline","url":"https://catalog.foothill.edu/course-outlines/C-S-1A/"},{"title":"Coast Community College District CS A170","url":"https://catalog.cccd.edu/courses/cs-a170/"},{"title":"Colorado State CS165 Practice Exam","url":"https://www.cs.colostate.edu/~cs165/.Fall19/studyguides/M1PracticeSol.pdf"},{"title":"Willamette CS141 Public Exam","url":"https://people.willamette.edu/~gorr/classes/cs141/study/Spring2018/exam2Sp2018cs141.pdf"},{"title":"CMU OLI Introduction to Programming in Java","url":"https://oli.cmu.edu/courses/introduction-to-programming-in-java-o-f/"},{"title":"MIT OpenCourseWare Introduction to Programming in Java","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-092-introduction-to-programming-in-java-january-iap-2010/"}],"priorities":["Methods, parameters, scope, and pass-by-value","Arrays, currentSize, bounds, and reference copies","Class design, encapsulation, constructors, and this","Loop bounds, nested loops, and code tracing","Integer division, type casting, and formatted output"]},"topics":[{"id":1,"unit":"Unit 0 - Course Initialization","topic":"Topic 1 - Introduction to CS","must":["Distinguish syntax, semantics, and case sensitivity.","Recognize compile-time/syntax errors versus logic and runtime problems.","Know the conventional public static void main(String[] args) entry point and the role of a driver class."],"pitfall":"The instructor's July 23 example uses the modern compact-source void main() style; the mock exam follows the conventional main signature emphasized in the Topic 1 slides.","example":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, CS 111!\");\n    }\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":2,"unit":"Unit 1 - Programming Fundamentals","topic":"Topic 2 - Data and Basic Output","must":["Primitive types include int, double, char, and boolean; String is a class/reference type.","Understand variable declaration, initialization, and assignment; a final constant must be initialized when declared.","Use single quotes for char and double quotes for String; know escape sequences, concatenation, print, and println.","Apply legal identifier rules and the camelCase / UPPER_SNAKE_CASE naming conventions."],"pitfall":"String concatenation evaluates from left to right: 2 + 3 + \" cats\" produces \"5 cats\", while \"cats \" + 2 + 3 produces \"cats 23\".","example":"final double TAX_RATE = 0.0775;\nint quantity = 3;\ndouble price = 12.50;\nchar grade = 'A';\nString label = \"Subtotal\";","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":3,"unit":"Unit 1 - Programming Fundamentals","topic":"Topic 3 - Data Manipulation and Typecasting","must":["Know the arithmetic operators +, -, *, /, and %, plus precedence; integer division discards the fractional part.","Use shorthand assignment (+=, -=, *=, /=, %=) and increment/decrement (++ / --).","Understand numeric promotion/coercion and explicit casts within the course model char < int < double."],"pitfall":"double x = 5 / 2 performs integer division first, so x becomes 2.0. Write (double) 5 / 2 to produce 2.5.","example":"double wrong = 5 / 2;          // 2.0\ndouble right = (double) 5 / 2; // 2.5\nint remainder = 17 % 5;        // 2","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":4,"unit":"Unit 1 - Programming Fundamentals","topic":"Topic 4 - Formatted Output","must":["print does not add a newline, println does, and \\n can force a line break.","Use the printf format pattern %[flags][width][.precision]conversion.","Common conversions are %d, %f, %s, and %c; %n inserts a platform-specific newline.","System.out.printf prints directly; String.format returns a String."],"pitfall":"The conversion must match the argument type. Precision changes the display, not the value stored in the variable.","example":"double price = 12.5;\nSystem.out.printf(\"$%,.2f%n\", price);\nString report = String.format(\"Score: %d\", 95);","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":5,"unit":"Unit 1 - Programming Fundamentals","topic":"Topic 5 - Static Methods and Scope","must":["A method consists of a header and body; a non-void method must return a value of the declared type.","A static method or variable belongs to the class; a local variable's scope and lifetime are limited to its method call.","A local variable with the same name shadows a class variable; use ClassName.variable to access the hidden static variable."],"pitfall":"main is the program entry point, but a project requirement for an additional static method normally does not count main.","example":"static int count = 10;\n\nstatic int square(int n) {\n    int result = n * n;\n    return result;\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":6,"unit":"Unit 1 - Programming Fundamentals","topic":"Topic 6 - Parameters and Passing","must":["A parameter is a placeholder variable in a method definition; an argument is the actual value or expression supplied in a call.","Java passes primitive values by value; changing a parameter does not change the caller's primitive variable.","A method should do one focused job; its return type describes the result, not the input.","Formal documentation includes a description, precondition, and postcondition; a test case includes the call, arguments, and expected outcome."],"pitfall":"Argument order and types must match the parameters. A method ends immediately when return executes.","example":"static void change(int value) {\n    value = 99;\n}\n\nint x = 8;\nchange(x); // x is still 8","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":7,"unit":"Unit 2 - Program Flow + Data Structures Intro","topic":"Topic 7 - Selection (Branching)","must":["Know the relational operators and the difference between == (comparison) and = (assignment).","Trace if, if-else, nested if, and else-if chains; after the first true branch runs, the remaining branches are skipped.","Use the logical operators &&, ||, and !, including short-circuit evaluation.","Know the ternary operator; use switch for equality checks on one expression, and understand break and fall-through."],"pitfall":"Java does not allow 90 <= score <= 100. Write score >= 90 && score <= 100.","example":"if (score >= 90 && score <= 100) {\n    grade = 'A';\n} else if (score >= 80) {\n    grade = 'B';\n} else {\n    grade = 'C';\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":8,"unit":"Unit 2 - Program Flow + Data Structures Intro","topic":"Topic 8 - Repetition (Looping)","must":["Every loop control variable must be initialized, checked, and updated.","while is a pre-test loop; for is useful when the iteration count is known. Know counter, accumulator, sentinel, and iteration.","Debug infinite loops, loops that never run, loops that run once, and off-by-one errors."],"pitfall":"for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) runs n + 1 times. Whether the final condition includes an equals sign is a frequent source of mistakes.","example":"int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {\n    total += i;\n}\n// 4 iterations; total is 10","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":9,"unit":"Unit 2 - Program Flow + Data Structures Intro","topic":"Topic 9 - Introduction to Arrays","must":["Declare, create, and initialize arrays; indices start at 0 and the last valid index is length - 1.","Know default values, .length, and ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.","Traverse arrays to compute sum, average, minimum, and maximum; track currentSize for a partially filled array.","Understand parallel arrays and varargs: one element type, at most one varargs parameter, and it must come last."],"pitfall":"array.length is capacity, not necessarily the number of valid elements. Process only [0, currentSize) in a partially filled array.","example":"int[] values = {3, 5, 7};\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {\n    sum += values[i];\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":10,"unit":"Unit 2 - Program Flow + Data Structures Intro","topic":"Topic 10 - Advanced Looping and Arrays","must":["Use enhanced for when no index is needed and the entire array should be processed.","do-while is a post-test loop that runs at least once; nested-loop iteration counts multiply.","break exits the entire loop; continue skips the rest of the current iteration.","For a 2-D array, array.length is the row count and array[row].length is that row's column count.","Understand references, the heap, shallow copies, and deep copies."],"pitfall":"b = a copies only the array reference, so changing an element through b also changes a. A true deep copy creates a new array and copies the elements.","example":"int[] a = {1, 2, 3};\nint[] alias = a;           // shallow/reference copy\nint[] copy = a.clone();    // separate array\n\nfor (int value : copy) {\n    System.out.println(value);\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":11,"unit":"Unit 3 - Intro to Object-Oriented Programming","topic":"Topic 11 - Intro to Objects/Classes + Strings","must":["A class is a blueprint and user-defined data type; an object is an instance of a class.","Know common String methods: length, substring, equals, equalsIgnoreCase, charAt, and indexOf.","Call a non-static method with object.method(); an instance method can access instance variables.","An object variable stores a reference; a method receives a copy of that reference value."],"pitfall":"Use equals, not ==, to compare String contents. A field change to the shared object is visible to the caller, but reassigning the parameter reference is not.","example":"String name = \"Java\";\nboolean same = name.equals(\"Java\");\nchar second = name.charAt(1); // 'a'\n\nDog dog = new Dog();\ndog.setName(\"Milo\");","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":12,"unit":"Unit 3 - Intro to Object-Oriented Programming","topic":"Topic 12 - Designing Objects/Classes","must":["Understand abstraction, information hiding, and encapsulation; instance variables are normally private.","Use public getters/accessors and setters/mutators; this.field resolves shadowing.","toString supplies an object's String representation and is called automatically when the object is printed.","equals compares object contents; in UML, - means private and + means public."],"pitfall":"A validating setter may return boolean. setAll should reuse the individual setters instead of duplicating validation logic.","example":"class Dog {\n    private int age;\n\n    public int getAge() { return age; }\n    public void setAge(int age) {\n        this.age = age;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return \"Dog age=\" + age;\n    }\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":13,"unit":"Unit 3 - Intro to Object-Oriented Programming","topic":"Topic 13 - Overloading and Constructors","must":["A constructor has the same name as its class and no return type—not even void.","Know default/no-arg, full, and copy constructors; this(...) must be the first constructor statement.","A copy constructor should create an independent object; an anonymous object is not stored in a reference variable.","A method signature is the method name plus parameter types; an overload has the same name but a different parameter list."],"pitfall":"Return type alone cannot distinguish overloaded methods. Watch for automatic conversions that make a call ambiguous.","example":"class Dog {\n    private String name;\n\n    public Dog() { this(\"Unknown\"); }\n    public Dog(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public Dog(Dog other) { this(other.name); }\n}","confidence":"confirmed"},{"id":14,"unit":"Unit 3 - Intro to Object-Oriented Programming","topic":"Topic 14 - Console Input + Validation (Possible Coverage)","must":["Use Scanner for basic console input and repeat until the input satisfies a range or format requirement.","A validation loop condition normally means “continue while invalid,” such as n < 1 || n > 10."],"pitfall":"Canvas says this work is due before 8:30 AM on exam day, while OLI currently shows 0% progress. At minimum, master basic Scanner input and validation loops before the exam.","example":"Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);\nint n;\ndo {\n    System.out.print(\"Enter 1-10: \");\n    n = input.nextInt();\n} while (n < 1 || n > 10);","confidence":"possible"},{"id":15,"unit":"Unit 3 - Intro to Object-Oriented Programming","topic":"Topic 15 - Inheritance, Overriding, Polymorphism (Low Confidence)","must":["extends expresses inheritance; a subclass can override an inherited instance method.","A base-type reference can point to a subclass object; the overridden method is selected at run time through polymorphism."],"pitfall":"Canvas still shows a 2025 due date and OLI shows 0% progress, so the current pages do not clearly confirm whether this topic is included on this final.","example":"class Animal {\n    public String speak() { return \"...\"; }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    public String speak() { return \"woof\"; }\n}\nAnimal pet = new Dog();\nSystem.out.println(pet.speak()); // woof","confidence":"low"}],"questions":[{"id":1,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Errors","prompt":"Which line contains a compile-time (syntax) error?","answer":"B","explanation":"The println statement is missing its semicolon. Choice C compiles but can fail at run time because n is zero.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["System.out.println(\"Hello\");","System.out.println(\"Hello\")","int n = 0; System.out.println(5 / n);","System.out.println(2 + 2);"]},{"id":2,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Java entry point","prompt":"Which is the conventional main method header emphasized in the Topic 1 slides?","answer":"B","explanation":"The course slides use public static void main(String[] args) as the standard Java entry point.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["public void main()","public static void main(String[] args)","static int main(String args)","public static String main()"]},{"id":3,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Data types","prompt":"Which type is the best choice for a price that may include cents?","answer":"B","explanation":"A double stores floating-point values such as 19.95.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["int","double","char","boolean"]},{"id":4,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 char vs String","prompt":"Which is a valid Java char literal?","answer":"B","explanation":"A char holds one character and uses single quotes. Double quotes create a String.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["\"A\"","'A'","\"AB\"","'AB'"]},{"id":5,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Identifiers","prompt":"Which identifier follows Java naming rules and the course camelCase convention?","answer":"D","explanation":"Identifiers cannot start with a digit, use a keyword, or contain a hyphen.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["2ndScore","class","days-until-final","daysUntilFinal"]},{"id":6,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Constants","prompt":"Which declaration correctly creates a constant?","answer":"D","explanation":"Java constants use final, include a type, and must be initialized when declared.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["double TAX_RATE;","const double TAX_RATE = 0.08;","final TAX_RATE = 0.08;","final double TAX_RATE = 0.08;"]},{"id":7,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Concatenation","prompt":"What is printed by System.out.println(2 + 3 + \" cats\");?","answer":"B","explanation":"Evaluation is left-to-right. The two integers are added before the String is encountered.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["23 cats","5 cats","cats 23","A compile-time error"]},{"id":8,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Integer division","prompt":"What is the value of 7 / 2 when both operands are int?","answer":"B","explanation":"Integer division truncates the fractional part.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["3.5","3","4","2"]},{"id":9,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Remainder","prompt":"What is the value of 17 % 5?","answer":"C","explanation":"17 divided by 5 leaves a remainder of 2.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["0","1","2","3"]},{"id":10,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Coercion","prompt":"After double x = 5 / 2;, what value is stored in x?","answer":"B","explanation":"5 / 2 is evaluated first as integer division (2), then widened to double (2.0).","difficulty":"standard","choices":["2.5","2.0","3.0","A compile-time error"]},{"id":11,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Casting","prompt":"What is the value of (double) 5 / 2?","answer":"C","explanation":"Casting one operand to double forces floating-point division.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["2","2.0","2.5","3.0"]},{"id":12,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 char arithmetic","prompt":"What character is produced by (char) ('A' + 2)?","answer":"C","explanation":"'A' is promoted to its numeric code, 2 is added, and the result is cast back to char.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["'A'","'B'","'C'","A compile-time error"]},{"id":13,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 printf","prompt":"What does System.out.printf(\"%.2f\", 3.14159); display?","answer":"A","explanation":"%.2f displays a floating-point value with two digits after the decimal point.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["3.14","3.14159","3","A compile-time error"]},{"id":14,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 String.format","prompt":"Which statement about String.format is correct?","answer":"C","explanation":"String.format builds and returns a String; printf prints immediately.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["It can format only integers.","It always prints immediately.","It returns a formatted String that can be stored.","It changes the original numeric variable."]},{"id":15,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Static methods","prompt":"A static method belongs to:","answer":"C","explanation":"Static members belong to the class and are commonly called with ClassName.method().","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["one specific object only","the local variable that calls it","the class itself","the operating system"]},{"id":16,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Scope","prompt":"When does a local variable normally stop existing?","answer":"B","explanation":"A local variable's lifetime is limited to the active method invocation.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["When the class is compiled","When its method call finishes","When any other method starts","Only when the computer shuts down"]},{"id":17,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Shadowing","prompt":"Inside Main, a local variable named count shadows static Main.count. Which expression explicitly accesses the static variable?","answer":"C","explanation":"The class name qualifies the shadowed static variable.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["this.count","count()","Main.count","static.count"]},{"id":18,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Return values","prompt":"Which rule applies to a non-void method?","answer":"B","explanation":"The return type is a promise that the method will provide a compatible value.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["It may never use return.","It must return a compatible value on every possible path.","It must print its result.","It cannot have parameters."]},{"id":19,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Parameters","prompt":"The actual value or expression supplied in a method call is called a(n):","answer":"B","explanation":"Parameters appear in the definition; arguments appear in the call.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["parameter","argument","return type","method header"]},{"id":20,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Pass by value","prompt":"What is printed?","answer":"B","explanation":"A copy of n's value is passed. Changing x does not change n.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["3","4","5","A compile-time error"],"code":"static void addOne(int x) { x++; }\nint n = 4;\naddOne(n);\nSystem.out.print(n);"},{"id":21,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Method design","prompt":"According to the course design guidance, a well-designed method should generally:","answer":"B","explanation":"Single-purpose methods are easier to understand, test, and reuse.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["perform as many unrelated tasks as possible","do one thing and do it well","use only String parameters","always be void"]},{"id":22,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Testing","prompt":"A method test case should include:","answer":"D","explanation":"The course defines a test case around a specific call and its expected result.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["only the method name","only the expected value","only a screenshot","a method call/arguments and the expected outcome"]},{"id":23,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Comparison","prompt":"Which operator tests whether two primitive values are equal?","answer":"B","explanation":"== compares; = assigns.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["=","==","!=","=>"]},{"id":24,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Boolean ranges","prompt":"Which condition correctly checks whether score is between 90 and 100 inclusive?","answer":"C","explanation":"Java requires two complete comparisons joined with &&.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["90 <= score <= 100","score >= 90 || score <= 100","score >= 90 && score <= 100","score == 90 && 100"]},{"id":25,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Short circuit","prompt":"If x is 0, what happens when Java evaluates x != 0 && 10 / x > 2?","answer":"B","explanation":"For &&, Java stops as soon as it knows the whole expression is false.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["It divides by zero.","It stops after the first false expression and the result is false.","It changes x to 1.","It produces true."]},{"id":26,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 else-if","prompt":"In an if / else if / else chain, what happens after the first true condition?","answer":"B","explanation":"Conditions are checked in order; only the first matching branch executes.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["Every later branch also runs.","The matching branch runs and the remaining branches are skipped.","Only else runs.","The program always exits."]},{"id":27,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 switch","prompt":"What is the usual effect of omitting break at the end of a matching switch case?","answer":"C","explanation":"This is switch fall-through.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["The switch cannot compile.","The switch restarts.","Execution falls through into following cases until a break or the end.","The default case is skipped automatically."]},{"id":28,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Ternary","prompt":"Which expression is equivalent to choosing \"adult\" when age >= 18 and \"minor\" otherwise?","answer":"B","explanation":"The ternary form is condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["age >= 18 : \"adult\" ? \"minor\"","age >= 18 ? \"adult\" : \"minor\"","if age >= 18 then \"adult\"","\"adult\" && \"minor\""]},{"id":29,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 Loop control","prompt":"Which missing loop-control step most commonly causes an infinite loop?","answer":"B","explanation":"Without an update that moves toward a false condition, the loop may never terminate.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["Declaring a constant","Updating the loop-control variable","Printing a newline","Using an accumulator"]},{"id":30,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 for loops","prompt":"Which loop is normally preferred when the exact number of repetitions is known?","answer":"A","explanation":"A for loop packages initialization, condition, and update for count-controlled repetition.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["for","while","do-while","switch"]},{"id":31,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 Off by one","prompt":"How many times does for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) execute its body?","answer":"C","explanation":"The values are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5: six iterations.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["4","5","6","7"]},{"id":32,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 Accumulator","prompt":"A variable that keeps a running total is called a(n):","answer":"B","explanation":"An accumulator combines values across iterations.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["sentinel","accumulator","iterator","parameter"]},{"id":33,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 do-while","prompt":"What guarantee does a do-while loop provide?","answer":"C","explanation":"The condition is checked after the body.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["It never runs.","It runs exactly twice.","Its body runs at least once.","It cannot be infinite."]},{"id":34,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 break/continue","prompt":"Which description is correct?","answer":"D","explanation":"break terminates the loop, while continue moves to the next iteration.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["break skips one statement; continue exits the program","break restarts the loop; continue exits the loop","break and continue always do the same thing","break exits the loop; continue skips the rest of the current iteration"]},{"id":35,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 Nested loops","prompt":"How many times is the inner statement executed?","answer":"C","explanation":"The outer loop runs 3 times and the inner loop runs 2 times per outer iteration: 3 x 2 = 6.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["2","3","6","9"],"code":"for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {\n    for (int c = 0; c < 2; c++) {\n        System.out.print(\"*\");\n    }\n}"},{"id":36,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Array indices","prompt":"For an array with length 4, what is the last valid index?","answer":"B","explanation":"Array indices run from 0 through length - 1.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["4","3","2","1"]},{"id":37,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Default values","prompt":"What is the default value of each element in new int[4]?","answer":"A","explanation":"Numeric primitive array elements are initialized to zero.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["0","1","null","undefined"]},{"id":38,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Bounds","prompt":"What happens when code accesses values[values.length]?","answer":"C","explanation":"values.length is one past the last valid index.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["It accesses the last element.","It creates a new element.","It causes an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at run time.","It always returns 0."]},{"id":39,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Average","prompt":"If sum and values.length are int, which expression preserves a fractional average?","answer":"C","explanation":"Casting before division forces floating-point arithmetic.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["sum / values.length","(int) sum / values.length","(double) sum / values.length","sum % values.length"]},{"id":40,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Partially filled arrays","prompt":"Besides the array itself, what must a program track for a partially filled array?","answer":"B","explanation":"The array length is capacity; a separate currentSize tracks valid entries.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["The compiler version","The number of elements currently in use","The number of methods in the class","The array's memory address as an int"]},{"id":41,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Parallel arrays","prompt":"In parallel arrays, related values are normally connected by:","answer":"C","explanation":"For example, names[i] and gpas[i] describe the same student.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["having different lengths","using different index positions","sharing the same index position","being stored in the same variable"]},{"id":42,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Varargs","prompt":"Which rule applies to a Java varargs parameter such as double... values?","answer":"D","explanation":"Ordinary parameters may come first, but the single varargs parameter must be last.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["A method may have unlimited varargs parameters.","It must be the first parameter.","It can mix unrelated element types.","There can be at most one, and it must be the last parameter."]},{"id":43,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 Enhanced for","prompt":"When is an enhanced for loop usually the best fit?","answer":"B","explanation":"The enhanced loop is concise when only element values are needed across the full array.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["When you must change the array length","When you need every value but do not need the index","When the array is partially filled and currentSize is smaller than length","When you must iterate backward by index"]},{"id":44,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 2-D arrays","prompt":"For int[][] grid, what do grid.length and grid[0].length usually represent?","answer":"B","explanation":"A 2-D array is an array of row arrays.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["columns, then rows","rows, then columns in row 0","total cells, then total bytes","last row index, then last column index"]},{"id":45,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 References","prompt":"After int[] b = a;, what kind of copy has been made?","answer":"B","explanation":"Only the reference value is copied. Both variables refer to the same array object.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["A deep copy of every element","A reference/alias copy, so a and b point to the same array","No copy and b is null","A compile-time copy that cannot be changed"]},{"id":46,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 String methods","prompt":"Which expression compares the contents of two String objects?","answer":"B","explanation":"equals compares String contents; == compares reference identity.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["a == b","a.equals(b)","a = b","equals(a, b)"]},{"id":47,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 String indexing","prompt":"What does \"Java\".charAt(1) return?","answer":"B","explanation":"String indices start at 0; index 1 is the char 'a'.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["'J'","'a'","'v'","\"a\""]},{"id":48,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 Classes","prompt":"A class is best described as:","answer":"C","explanation":"Objects are instances of the class.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["one specific object","a loop that creates variables","a template/custom data type used to create objects","a primitive value"]},{"id":49,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 Instance methods","prompt":"If dog is a Dog object with a non-static getName method, which call is correct?","answer":"A","explanation":"Non-static methods are invoked on a specific object using dot notation.","difficulty":"foundational","choices":["dog.getName()","Dog->getName()","getName.Dog()","static dog.getName()"]},{"id":50,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 Object parameters","prompt":"When an object is passed to a Java method, which statement is accurate?","answer":"C","explanation":"Java is pass-by-value, including when the value being copied is an object reference.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["Java passes the whole object by reference with no copy of any value.","Java always deep-copies the object.","Java passes a copy of the reference value; field mutations can be visible, but reassigning the parameter does not reassign the caller's variable.","Objects cannot be method arguments."]},{"id":51,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Code trace","prompt":"What is the exact output?","answer":"A","explanation":"x uses integer division before widening; y casts before division.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["3.0 3.5","3.5 3.5","3 3.5","3.0 3.0"],"code":"int a = 7;\nint b = 2;\ndouble x = a / b;\ndouble y = (double) a / b;\nSystem.out.println(x + \" \" + y);"},{"id":52,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Shadowing trace","prompt":"Assume this code is inside class Main. What is the exact output?","answer":"A","explanation":"The local n shadows the static n; Main.n explicitly accesses the class variable.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["3 10","10 3","3 3","10 10"],"code":"static int n = 10;\n\nstatic void show() {\n    int n = 3;\n    System.out.print(n + \" \" + Main.n);\n}"},{"id":53,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Pass by value trace","prompt":"What is the exact output?","answer":"B","explanation":"The method changes only its local copy of the primitive value.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["99","8","0","A compile-time error"],"code":"static void change(int value) {\n    value = 99;\n}\n\nint score = 8;\nchange(score);\nSystem.out.print(score);"},{"id":54,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Branch trace","prompt":"What is the exact output?","answer":"A","explanation":"-2 follows the negative branch; 0 follows the final else branch.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["Negative,Zero","Positive,Zero","Negative,Negative","Zero,Zero"],"code":"static String classify(int n) {\n    if (n > 0) return \"Positive\";\n    else if (n < 0) return \"Negative\";\n    else return \"Zero\";\n}\n\nSystem.out.print(classify(-2) + \",\" + classify(0));"},{"id":55,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 Loop trace","prompt":"Which pair correctly gives the iteration count and printed value?","answer":"B","explanation":"The loop adds 1 + 2 + 3 + 4.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["3 iterations; 6","4 iterations; 10","5 iterations; 10","4 iterations; 6"],"code":"int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {\n    total += i;\n}\nSystem.out.print(total);"},{"id":56,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Array trace","prompt":"What numeric value is printed?","answer":"C","explanation":"The sum is 19, and 19.0 / 4 is 4.75.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["4","4.0","4.75","5.0"],"code":"int[] values = {4, 7, 1, 7};\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int value : values) {\n    sum += value;\n}\nSystem.out.print((double) sum / values.length);"},{"id":57,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 2-D trace","prompt":"What is the exact output?","answer":"D","explanation":"The nested loops visit and add all six elements.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["6","15","18","21"],"code":"int[][] m = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int r = 0; r < m.length; r++) {\n    for (int c = 0; c < m[r].length; c++) {\n        sum += m[r][c];\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.print(sum);"},{"id":58,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Constructors","prompt":"Why is public void Dog() not a constructor, and which corrected header is valid?","answer":"B","explanation":"Constructors have the class name and no return type, including no void.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["It is static; use static Dog().","It has a return type; use public Dog().","It has no parameters; use public void Dog(int age).","Dog must be private; use private void Dog()."]},{"id":59,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 Encapsulation","prompt":"Which getter/setter pair correctly encapsulates private int age; and handles shadowing?","answer":"C","explanation":"The getter returns the field, and this.age identifies the instance variable inside the setter.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["public void getAge() { return age; } public int setAge(int age) { age = age; }","private int getAge() { return this; } public void setAge(int age) { age = this.age; }","public int getAge() { return age; } public void setAge(int age) { this.age = age; }","public static int getAge() { return age; } public static void setAge(int age) { this.age = age; }"]},{"id":60,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 toString","prompt":"If Dog correctly overrides toString(), which method is automatically requested by System.out.println(dog);?","answer":"B","explanation":"println automatically requests the object's String representation.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["dog.equals()","dog.toString()","Dog.print()","System.toString(dog)"]},{"id":61,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"Teacher sample / arrays + classes","prompt":"In Course.addStudent, what does roster[currentSize++] = student; do?","answer":"C","explanation":"The old value is used as the index before currentSize changes.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["Increment currentSize first, then store at the new index.","Store at index currentSize and leave currentSize unchanged.","Store at the old currentSize index, then increment currentSize.","Store in every unused array slot."]},{"id":62,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"Teacher sample / capacity","prompt":"Which guard should Course.addStudent use before roster[currentSize++] = student; to avoid writing past a full array?","answer":"A","explanation":"When currentSize equals length, there is no valid slot left.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["if (currentSize >= roster.length) return false;","if (currentSize <= roster.length) return false;","if (roster.length > 0) return false;","if (currentSize == 0) return false;"]},{"id":63,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Constructor chaining/copy","prompt":"Which statement correctly combines the rule for this(...) and the goal of a copy constructor?","answer":"D","explanation":"Constructor chaining centralizes initialization; copy construction should not alias the same mutable object.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["this(...) must be last; a copy constructor creates an alias.","this(...) may appear anywhere; a copy constructor returns the original object.","this(...) must be first; a copy constructor makes both variables reference the same object.","this(...) must be first; a copy constructor initializes a separate object from another object's data."]},{"id":64,"type":"mc","topicId":14,"topic":"T14 Validation - possible coverage","prompt":"A do-while loop should repeat while n is outside the valid range 1 through 10. Which condition is correct?","answer":"B","explanation":"Repeat when n violates either the lower or upper bound.","difficulty":"standard","choices":["n >= 1 && n <= 10","n < 1 || n > 10","n < 1 && n > 10","n == 1 || n == 10"]},{"id":65,"type":"mc","topicId":15,"topic":"T15 Polymorphism - low confidence","prompt":"What is printed, and which OOP idea explains it?","answer":"C","explanation":"The reference type is Animal, but the actual object is Dog, so the overridden Dog method runs.","difficulty":"challenge","choices":["?; compile-time binding","?; constructor overloading","woof; overriding and runtime polymorphism","woof; method overloading only"],"code":"class Animal {\n    String speak() { return \"?\"; }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    String speak() { return \"woof\"; }\n}\nAnimal a = new Dog();\nSystem.out.print(a.speak());"},{"id":66,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Syntax and semantics","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"What does Java's case sensitivity mean?","choices":["NAME and name are the same identifier","NAME and name are different identifiers","Java ignores capitalization in method names","Only class names are case-sensitive"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Java treats uppercase and lowercase letters as different characters in identifiers."},{"id":67,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Program structure","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Which method header is the conventional entry point for a Java application?","choices":["public void main()","static void main(String args)","public static void main(String[] args)","public static int main(String[] args)"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The conventional entry point is public static void main(String[] args)."},{"id":68,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Error classification","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"A program compiles, but evaluating 10 / 0 stops it during execution. How should the problem be classified?","choices":["Syntax error","Compile-time type error","Runtime error","Formatting error"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Integer division by zero is detected while the program runs, so it is a runtime error."},{"id":69,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Primitive types","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Which declaration stores a true/false value?","choices":["int ready = 1;","boolean ready = true;","String ready = true;","char ready = \"T\";"],"answer":"B","explanation":"boolean is the primitive type for true and false."},{"id":70,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Concatenation","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"What is printed by System.out.println(\"Total: \" + 2 + 3);?","choices":["Total: 5","Total: 23","5Total:","A compile-time error"],"answer":"B","explanation":"After the String appears, + performs left-to-right String concatenation, so 2 and 3 are appended separately."},{"id":71,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Constants","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"Which statement about final local variables is correct?","choices":["They can be reassigned once","They must always be named in camelCase","After initialization, they cannot be assigned a new value","They automatically become static"],"answer":"C","explanation":"final prevents reassignment after initialization; naming and static membership are separate concerns."},{"id":72,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Integer division","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"What is the value of 9 / 4 when both operands are int?","choices":["2","2.0","2.25","3"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Integer division discards the fractional portion, producing 2."},{"id":73,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Casting order","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Which expression evaluates to 3.5?","choices":["(double) (7 / 2)","(double) 7 / 2","7 / (int) 2.0","7 / 2"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Casting 7 before division promotes the operation to floating point. Casting after 7 / 2 would preserve the truncated value 3."},{"id":74,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Increment trace","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"After this code runs, what are n and result?","choices":["n = 7, result = 3.5","n = 8, result = 3.5","n = 8, result = 4.0","n = 7, result = 4.0"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Post-increment supplies 7 to the division, then changes n to 8. Dividing by 2.0 produces 3.5.","code":"int n = 7;\ndouble result = n++ / 2.0;"},{"id":75,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 printf precision","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"What does %.2f request when formatting a double?","choices":["A field width of 2","Two digits after the decimal point","Two total digits","Integer output"],"answer":"B","explanation":"For %f, .2 sets two digits after the decimal point."},{"id":76,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 Field width","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"What is the minimum field width requested by %6.1f?","choices":["1 character","5 characters","6 characters","7 characters"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The width value 6 specifies a minimum field width of six characters; .1 controls the fractional digits."},{"id":77,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 Formatting methods","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes printf from String.format?","choices":["Both return a formatted String","printf prints output; String.format returns a String","String.format prints output; printf only validates it","Neither supports %s"],"answer":"B","explanation":"System.out.printf writes formatted output, while String.format constructs and returns formatted text."},{"id":78,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Local scope","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Where can a local variable declared inside a method be used?","choices":["Anywhere in the class","Only within its scope in that method","Only from main","From every static method"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A local variable exists and is visible only within its declared scope."},{"id":79,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Static access","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Which is the clearest way to access a static field named count from outside its class Counter?","choices":["this.count","Counter.count","new count","count()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Static members belong to the class and are conventionally accessed with ClassName.member."},{"id":80,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Shadowing","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["3","10","13","A compile-time error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The local variable count shadows the static field, so the unqualified name refers to the local value 3.","code":"static int count = 10;\nstatic void show() {\n    int count = 3;\n    System.out.print(count);\n}"},{"id":81,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Parameters and arguments","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"In square(5), what is 5 called?","choices":["A return type","A parameter","An argument","A method header"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The value supplied at the call site is an argument; the placeholder in the method definition is a parameter."},{"id":82,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Pass-by-value","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"A method assigns 99 to an int parameter. What happens to the caller's int variable?","choices":["It always becomes 99","It is unchanged","It becomes null","The program cannot compile"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Java passes a copy of a primitive value, so assigning the parameter does not change the caller's variable."},{"id":83,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Return trace","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"What are n and result after this code?","choices":["n = 4, result = 8","n = 8, result = 8","n = 4, result = 4","n = 8, result = 4"],"answer":"A","explanation":"twice receives a copy of n, changes the copy, and returns 8. The caller's n remains 4.","code":"static int twice(int x) {\n    x *= 2;\n    return x;\n}\nint n = 4;\nint result = twice(n);"},{"id":84,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Comparison","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Which operator tests whether two primitive values are equal?","choices":["=","==","!=","=>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"== compares values; = performs assignment."},{"id":85,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Short-circuiting","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Which condition safely avoids division by zero?","choices":["n / d > 2 && d != 0","d != 0 && n / d > 2","d == 0 && n / d > 2","n / d > 2 || d != 0"],"answer":"B","explanation":"With &&, Java stops after the false left operand, so the division runs only when d is nonzero."},{"id":86,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 switch fall-through","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["2","23","3","default"],"answer":"B","explanation":"case 2 prints 2 and has no break, so execution falls through to case 3 and prints 3.","code":"int n = 2;\nswitch (n) {\n    case 2: System.out.print(\"2\");\n    case 3: System.out.print(\"3\"); break;\n    default: System.out.print(\"D\");\n}"},{"id":87,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 for-loop count","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"How many times does for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) run?","choices":["2","3","4","Infinitely"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The loop runs for i values 0, 1, and 2—three iterations."},{"id":88,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 do-while","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Why can a do-while loop be useful for an input prompt?","choices":["It cannot repeat","Its body runs at least once","It checks the condition before the body","It requires no condition"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A do-while loop is post-test, so the prompt body executes before the first condition check."},{"id":89,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 Nested-loop trace","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"How many times does count++ execute?","choices":["3","5","6","9"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The outer loop runs twice and the inner loop runs three times per outer iteration: 2 × 3 = 6.","code":"int count = 0;\nfor (int r = 0; r < 2; r++) {\n    for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\n        count++;\n    }\n}"},{"id":90,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Array indices","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"For an array with length 6, what is the last valid index?","choices":["5","6","7","It depends on the element type"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Array indices start at 0, so the last valid index is length - 1, or 5."},{"id":91,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Partial fills","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"An array has length 20 and currentSize is 7. Which indices contain the active elements?","choices":["0 through 7","1 through 7","0 through 6","7 through 19"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A currentSize of 7 means the active half-open range is [0, 7), or indices 0 through 6."},{"id":92,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Varargs","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"Which method header uses varargs legally?","choices":["static int sum(int... values, String label)","static int sum(int... a, int... b)","static int sum(String label, int... values)","static int... sum(int values)"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A method may have at most one varargs parameter, and it must be last."},{"id":93,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 Enhanced for","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"When is an enhanced for loop most appropriate?","choices":["When every element is processed and no index is needed","When the loop must run backward by index","When elements must be inserted","When only the first element is used"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Enhanced for is ideal for visiting each element when direct index control is unnecessary."},{"id":94,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 Two-dimensional arrays","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"For int[][] grid, what does grid[row].length report?","choices":["The number of rows","The number of columns in that row","The total number of elements","The last valid row index"],"answer":"B","explanation":"grid[row] is a one-dimensional array, so its length is the number of columns in that row."},{"id":95,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 References and copies","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"After int[] b = a; b[0] = 9;, what happens to a[0]?","choices":["It is unchanged","It becomes 9","It becomes null","The code cannot compile"],"answer":"B","explanation":"b = a makes both variables refer to the same array, so an element change through b is visible through a."},{"id":96,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 String equality","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Which expression compares the contents of two String variables a and b?","choices":["a == b","a.equals(b)","a = b","equals(a, b)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"equals compares String contents; == compares reference identity."},{"id":97,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 substring","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"What does \"Java\".substring(1, 3) return?","choices":["\"Jav\"","\"av\"","\"ava\"","\"a\""],"answer":"B","explanation":"substring includes the start index and excludes the end index, giving characters at indices 1 and 2."},{"id":98,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 Object parameters","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"A method receives a Dog reference and calls dog.setAge(5). Why can the caller observe the new age?","choices":["Objects are passed by reference","The copied reference still points to the same object","The method becomes static","setAge changes every Dog"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Java passes the reference value by value. Both reference copies can still point to and modify the same object."},{"id":99,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 Encapsulation","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"What access modifier is normally used for instance variables in an encapsulated class?","choices":["public","private","protected only","static"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Private fields protect an object's representation; public methods provide controlled access."},{"id":100,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 Validation","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Why should setAll reuse individual validating setters?","choices":["To make every field static","To avoid duplicating validation rules","To remove constructors","To expose private fields"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Reusing setters keeps validation in one place and prevents inconsistent duplicate logic."},{"id":101,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 UML visibility","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"In the course UML notation, what do -age:int and +getAge():int mean?","choices":["Both members are public","age is private and getAge is public","age is static and getAge is final","Both members are private"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A minus sign marks private visibility, while a plus sign marks public visibility."},{"id":102,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Constructor syntax","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Which statement about a constructor is true?","choices":["It must return void","Its name matches the class and it has no return type","It must be static","It can be called only once per class"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A constructor has the class name and no return type—not even void."},{"id":103,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Overloading","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"Which pair is a legal overload?","choices":["set(int x) and set(int y)","set(int x) and set(double x)","int set(int x) and double set(int x)","set(int x) and set(int x)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Overloads need different parameter type lists. Parameter names and return types do not distinguish signatures."},{"id":104,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Constructor chaining","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"Where must this(...) appear when one constructor calls another constructor in the same class?","choices":["Anywhere in the body","As the final statement","As the first statement","Only inside a static method"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A this(...) constructor call must be the first statement in the constructor."},{"id":105,"type":"mc","topicId":14,"topic":"T14 Scanner basics","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Which class is commonly used for console input in this course?","choices":["Printer","Scanner","ConsoleReader only","Formatter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"java.util.Scanner provides the console-input methods used in the course material."},{"id":106,"type":"mc","topicId":14,"topic":"T14 Range validation","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"To keep asking while n is outside 1 through 10, which condition should the loop use?","choices":["n >= 1 && n <= 10","n < 1 || n > 10","n < 1 && n > 10","n == 1 || n == 10"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The invalid range consists of values below 1 or above 10, so the condition uses ||."},{"id":107,"type":"mc","topicId":14,"topic":"T14 Input validation","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"Before calling nextInt(), which Scanner method can test whether the next token is an integer?","choices":["isInt()","hasNextInt()","nextIsInt()","validInt()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"hasNextInt() checks the upcoming token without consuming it."},{"id":108,"type":"mc","topicId":15,"topic":"T15 Inheritance","difficulty":"foundational","prompt":"Which Java keyword declares that Dog inherits from Animal?","choices":["implements","extends","inherits","superclass"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A class uses extends to inherit from another class."},{"id":109,"type":"mc","topicId":15,"topic":"T15 Overriding","difficulty":"standard","prompt":"What does @Override help the compiler verify?","choices":["That a field is private","That a method matches an inherited method signature","That a constructor returns void","That a class is static"],"answer":"B","explanation":"@Override asks the compiler to confirm that the method actually overrides an inherited method."},{"id":110,"type":"mc","topicId":15,"topic":"T15 Polymorphism","difficulty":"challenge","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["animal","dog","A compile-time error","Nothing"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The actual object is a Dog, so runtime dispatch selects Dog's overridden label method.","code":"class Animal { String label() { return \"animal\"; } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { @Override String label() { return \"dog\"; } }\nAnimal pet = new Dog();\nSystem.out.print(pet.label());"},{"id":111,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Definite assignment","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"Why does this program fail to compile?","choices":["main must return int","A local variable may be read before it is initialized","println cannot print int values","The braces are unbalanced"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Java's definite-assignment rule prevents a local variable from being read before every possible path assigns it.","code":"public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int score;\n    if (args.length > 0) score = 10;\n    System.out.println(score);\n}"},{"id":112,"type":"mc","topicId":1,"topic":"T1 Error diagnosis","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"Which diagnosis is correct?","choices":["The code has a syntax error on line 1","The code always prints 5","The code compiles, but the division can throw an ArithmeticException","The code cannot compile because denominator is an int"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The statements are syntactically valid. Because denominator is zero, integer division fails at run time.","code":"int denominator = 0;\nint result = 5 / denominator;\nSystem.out.println(result);"},{"id":113,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Mixed expressions","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["A1B2","66B2","66B3","A3B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'A' + 1 is numeric addition and produces 66. Once \"B\" is reached, the remaining + operations concatenate.","code":"System.out.println('A' + 1 + \"B\" + 2);"},{"id":114,"type":"mc","topicId":2,"topic":"T2 Evaluation order","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["sum=9.0","sum=45.0","sum=4.05","A compile-time error"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The expression starts with a String, so 4 and 5 are concatenated before \".0\" is appended.","code":"int a = 4;\nint b = 5;\nSystem.out.println(\"sum=\" + a + b + \".0\");"},{"id":115,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Compound assignment","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is the value of n after the assignment?","choices":["7","7.8","8","A compile-time error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Compound assignment includes an implicit cast back to the left operand's type, so 7.8 is narrowed to 7.","code":"int n = 5;\nn += 2.8;"},{"id":116,"type":"mc","topicId":3,"topic":"T3 Promotion trace","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["2.0 3","2.5 3","2.5 2","2.0 2"],"answer":"B","explanation":"x++ supplies 5 and then changes x to 6. Floating-point division gives 2.5, and 6 % 3 is 0, so the second expression is 3.","code":"int x = 5;\ndouble a = x++ / 2.0;\nint b = x / 2 + x % 3;\nSystem.out.println(a + \" \" + b);"},{"id":117,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 Width and zero padding","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What String is produced?","choices":["\"3.50\"","\"003.50\"","\"0003.50\"","\"  3.5\""],"answer":"B","explanation":"The field width is six characters, the 0 flag pads on the left, and .2 prints two fractional digits.","code":"String s = String.format(\"%06.2f\", 3.5);"},{"id":118,"type":"mc","topicId":4,"topic":"T4 Multiple conversions","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["id=07 avg=2.33","id=7 avg=2.3","id=07 avg=2.34","id=007 avg=2.33"],"answer":"A","explanation":"%02d pads the integer to two digits, and %.2f rounds 7.0 / 3 to 2.33.","code":"int id = 7;\ndouble avg = 7.0 / 3;\nSystem.out.printf(\"id=%02d avg=%.2f\", id, avg);"},{"id":119,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Shared static state","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["1 1","1 2","2 2","2 1"],"answer":"C","explanation":"count is static, so both objects observe the single class variable after two constructor calls.","code":"class Counter {\n    static int count = 0;\n    Counter() { count++; }\n}\nCounter a = new Counter();\nCounter b = new Counter();\nSystem.out.print(a.count + \" \" + b.count);"},{"id":120,"type":"mc","topicId":5,"topic":"T5 Shadowing and qualification","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["4 9 4","9 9 9","4 4 9","4 9 9"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The parameter n is 4, Main.n is the static field 9, and the call to read() returns that same static field.","code":"static int n = 9;\nstatic int read() { return n; }\nstatic void show(int n) {\n    System.out.print(n + \" \" + Main.n + \" \" + read());\n}\nshow(4);"},{"id":121,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Nested method calls","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["10","12","16","20"],"answer":"D","explanation":"twice(3) returns 6; add(6, 4) returns 10; twice(10) returns 20.","code":"static int twice(int x) { return x * 2; }\nstatic int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nSystem.out.print(twice(add(twice(3), 4)));"},{"id":122,"type":"mc","topicId":6,"topic":"T6 Reference-value passing","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["1","2","10","11"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The element mutation affects the caller's array. Reassigning the local parameter to a new array does not change the caller's reference.","code":"static void revise(int[] data) {\n    data[0]++;\n    data = new int[] {9};\n    data[0]++;\n}\nint[] values = {1};\nrevise(values);\nSystem.out.print(values[0]);"},{"id":123,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Else-if trace","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["AB","AC","BC","ABC"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The first independent if prints A. The second if is true and prints B, so its attached else is skipped.","code":"int x = 8;\nif (x > 5) System.out.print(\"A\");\nif (x % 2 == 0) System.out.print(\"B\");\nelse System.out.print(\"C\");"},{"id":124,"type":"mc","topicId":7,"topic":"T7 Short-circuit side effects","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["0 true","1 false","2 false","2 true"],"answer":"B","explanation":"x++ compares 0 > 0, which is false, then increments x to 1. && short-circuits, so ++x never executes.","code":"int x = 0;\nboolean ok = (x++ > 0) && (++x > 1);\nSystem.out.print(x + \" \" + ok);"},{"id":125,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 continue trace","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["4","6","9","12"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The continue skips even values. The loop adds 1 + 3 + 5 = 9.","code":"int sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n    if (i % 2 == 0) continue;\n    sum += i;\n}\nSystem.out.print(sum);"},{"id":126,"type":"mc","topicId":8,"topic":"T8 Triangular nested loop","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"How many times does count++ execute?","choices":["6","10","12","16"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The inner loop runs 1, 2, 3, and 4 times. The total is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10.","code":"int count = 0;\nfor (int row = 0; row < 4; row++) {\n    for (int col = 0; col <= row; col++) count++;\n}"},{"id":127,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Maximum algorithm","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"The method should return the largest value even when every value is negative. Which initialization fixes it?","choices":["int max = 0;","int max = values.length;","int max = values[0];","int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE + values[0];"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Initializing max to the first element makes the algorithm valid for all nonempty arrays, including all-negative inputs.","code":"static int max(int[] values) {\n    int max = 0;\n    for (int v : values) if (v > max) max = v;\n    return max;\n}"},{"id":128,"type":"mc","topicId":9,"topic":"T9 Partial-array insertion","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"After the insertion, what are the first four active values?","choices":["2, 4, 5, 6","2, 5, 4, 6","2, 5, 6, 4","4, 2, 5, 6"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Elements at and after index 1 shift right from the end toward the insertion point, then 5 is stored at index 1.","code":"int[] a = {2, 4, 6, 0, 0};\nint currentSize = 3;\nint index = 1;\nfor (int i = currentSize; i > index; i--) a[i] = a[i - 1];\na[index] = 5;\ncurrentSize++;"},{"id":129,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 Ragged arrays","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["3","6","9","A runtime error"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The enhanced loops visit every value in rows of different lengths: 1 + 2 + 3 + 3 = 9.","code":"int[][] grid = {{1, 2}, {3}, {3}};\nint total = 0;\nfor (int[] row : grid)\n    for (int value : row) total += value;\nSystem.out.print(total);"},{"id":130,"type":"mc","topicId":10,"topic":"T10 2-D shallow copy","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["1 9","9 9","1 1","A compile-time error"],"answer":"B","explanation":"clone creates a new outer array but copies the inner-array references, so a[0] and b[0] still refer to the same row.","code":"int[][] a = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};\nint[][] b = a.clone();\nb[0][0] = 9;\nSystem.out.print(a[0][0] + \" \" + b[0][0]);"},{"id":131,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 String chain","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["ava-3","av-2","Java-4","av-3"],"answer":"B","explanation":"substring(1,3) is \"av\" and indexOf('v') is 2.","code":"String s = \"Java\";\nSystem.out.print(s.substring(1, 3) + \"-\" + s.indexOf('v'));"},{"id":132,"type":"mc","topicId":11,"topic":"T11 Object alias trace","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["2 7","7 7","2 2","A compile-time error"],"answer":"B","explanation":"b is an alias for a, so changing b.value changes the one shared Box object.","code":"class Box { int value; Box(int value) { this.value = value; } }\nBox a = new Box(2);\nBox b = a;\nb.value = 7;\nSystem.out.print(a.value + \" \" + b.value);"},{"id":133,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 Static and instance fields","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["A:10 B:20","A:20 B:20","B:20 A:20","A:10 B:10"],"answer":"B","explanation":"name is an instance field, but size is static and shared. Changing size through one object changes what both objects report.","code":"class Bag {\n    static int size = 10;\n    String name;\n    Bag(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public String toString() { return name + \":\" + size; }\n}\nBag a = new Bag(\"A\");\nBag b = new Bag(\"B\");\na.size = 20;\nSystem.out.print(a + \" \" + b);"},{"id":134,"type":"mc","topicId":12,"topic":"T12 equals design","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"Which implementation best follows the course's content-equality goal for Dog?","choices":["return this == other;","return name == other.name && age == other.age;","return other != null && age == other.age && name.equals(other.name);","return toString() == other.toString();"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Content equality checks null, compares primitive fields with ==, and compares String contents with equals."},{"id":135,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Constructor chaining","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["1","2","3","A compile-time error"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Each constructed object ultimately runs the two-argument constructor once, which increments created. Three objects are constructed.","code":"class Pair {\n    static int created = 0;\n    Pair() { this(0, 0); }\n    Pair(int a) { this(a, 0); }\n    Pair(int a, int b) { created++; }\n}\nnew Pair(); new Pair(4); new Pair(4, 5);\nSystem.out.print(Pair.created);"},{"id":136,"type":"mc","topicId":13,"topic":"T13 Copy constructor aliasing","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"Why is this copy constructor not a deep copy?","choices":["It returns void","It copies the array reference, so both objects share the same scores array","It cannot access other.scores","Arrays cannot be fields"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Assigning scores = other.scores copies only the reference. A deep copy must allocate a new array and copy its elements.","code":"class Record {\n    private int[] scores;\n    Record(Record other) {\n        scores = other.scores;\n    }\n}"},{"id":137,"type":"mc","topicId":14,"topic":"T14 Scanner token validation","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"If the next input token is \"cat\", which branch safely consumes it without calling nextInt()?","choices":["The first branch","The else branch","Both branches","Neither branch"],"answer":"B","explanation":"hasNextInt() is false for \"cat\", so next() consumes the invalid token in the else branch.","code":"if (input.hasNextInt()) {\n    int n = input.nextInt();\n} else {\n    input.next();\n}"},{"id":138,"type":"mc","topicId":14,"topic":"T14 Validation logic","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"Which replacement accepts only integers from 1 through 10 and keeps asking for every other integer?","choices":["while (n >= 1 || n <= 10)","while (n < 1 && n > 10)","while (n < 1 || n > 10)","while (n != 1 && n != 10)"],"answer":"C","explanation":"An integer is invalid when it is below 1 or above 10, so the loop must use ||."},{"id":139,"type":"mc","topicId":15,"topic":"T15 Dynamic dispatch","difficulty":"standard","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed?","choices":["Animal Animal Dog","Animal Dog Dog","Dog Dog Dog","Animal Dog Animal"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first object is Animal. The next two actual objects are Dog, so their overridden label methods run.","code":"class Animal { String label() { return \"Animal\"; } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { String label() { return \"Dog\"; } }\nAnimal a = new Animal();\nAnimal b = new Dog();\nDog c = new Dog();\nSystem.out.print(a.label() + \" \" + b.label() + \" \" + c.label());"},{"id":140,"type":"mc","topicId":15,"topic":"T15 super and overriding","difficulty":"challenge","sourceStyle":"public-college","prompt":"What is printed by t.m2();?","choices":["car-m1","truck-m1","car-m2","truck-m2"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Truck.m2 explicitly calls super.m1(), so Car's implementation runs even though the object is a Truck.","code":"class Car {\n    void m1() { System.out.print(\"car-m1\"); }\n}\nclass Truck extends Car {\n    @Override void m1() { System.out.print(\"truck-m1\"); }\n    void m2() { super.m1(); }\n}\nTruck t = new Truck();"}]};
